@ 15.2.1 - Proto-Eskimo to Barrow Iñupiaq
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Swadesh, Morris (1952), “Unaaliq and Proto Eskimo IV: Diachronic Notes”, International Journal of American Linguistics, Vol. 18, No. 3 (Jul., 1952), pp. 166–171 Regressive MOA assimilation and progressive voicing assimilation in consonant clusters (at least, when C₂ is either /l/ or /l̥/)
	l̥ > l / medial
	t > s / i_
	ə > i / at word boundaries
	ə > u / u_
	ə > a / a_
	ə > ∅ / else
	ɣ ʁ > k q / _#
	CVC > C:[+long]
	m n ŋ > v t ɣ / _C:[-nasal]
	l̥ > t / _C
	v > p / _s
	v ʁ > p q / S_
	v ɣ ʁ > p k q / _C (unless C = one of /l d͡ʒ m n ŋ)
	{p,v} t {k,ɣ} > m n ŋ / _N
	v > ∅ / u_i
	d͡ʒ > ʁ / i_u
	əd͡ʒ > i / _{a,u} (except in #U)
	ad͡ʒ > i / _a (except in #U?)